  <iati-identifier>GB-GOV-10-AHPSR-Webinars</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="GB-GOV-10" type="10">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">UK - Department of Health and Social Care (DHSC)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The state of health policy and systems research: reflections to inform future evolution</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">1) Explore topical areas that Health Policy and Systems Research (HPSR) has traditionally not focused on and where it has the greatest potential to contribute to improved health and well-being, and identify both broad thematic areas as well as priority research questions related to these areas where HPSR is uniquely positioned to add value to what is already known.  
2) Identify the potential range of new methods that HPSR can adopt in a rapidly changing world that will enable it to meet ever evolving challenges to human and planetary health and well-being. 
3) Explore how best to conduct HPSR to enable the necessary power shift from global to national actors and from researchers to policy-makers, practitioners and communities. 
4) Delivere a series of three webinars to reflect on current issues of importance in HPSR.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">A collaboration between the World Health Organization (WHO) Alliance for Health Policy and Systems Research (AHPSR) and the National Institute for Health and Care Research (NIHR) to support the Alliance and Low- and Middle-Income Country (LMIC) researchers and academics to collaboratively develop a set of papers involving a wide range of stakeholders who have been key to growth of the global Health Policy and Systems Research field to date, as well as emerging LMIC health policy and systems researchers, practitioners and thinkers who will play a major role in charting its future evolution. The Alliance will also support a series of webinars demonstrating the value add of HPSR for global health and new growth areas in the field.</narrative>
  </description>
